// ---------------------------------------------------------- the short guide
// All the model knows about Kosika is what is written here.

fn guide() -> Text {
  return "You write programs in Kosika, a small programming language. Kosika is NOT Python, JavaScript or Rust. Use only what is shown here.

RULES
- Types are Text, Number and Bool. A list is written Number[] or Text[].
- Data is a record. Fields are separated by spaces, never commas: record Order { item: Text price: Number }
- A record value is written {item: "tea", price: 3}. Read a field with order.price.
- A function: fn area(box: Box) -> Number { return box.w * box.h }
- let x = 1 cannot change. var total = 0 can change: total = total + 1
- if a > b { ... } else if a == b { ... } else { ... }
- Operators: + - * / == != < > <= >= and or not
- Loops: for item in list { ... }   for i in 0 until n { ... }   while count < 10 { ... }
- Lists: [1, 2, 3]   list.length()   list[0]   list.contains(x)   list.reverse()   list.is_empty()   list.take(2) is the first two   list.drop(1) is all but the first
- Adding to a list makes a new list: items = items.append(x)
- An empty list needs its type: var items: Number[] = []
- Text: a + b joins two texts, text.length(), text.upper(), text.lower(), text.trim(), text.contains("x"), text.split(" "), parts.join(" "), number.to_text()
- More text: text.words() splits on spaces, text.replace("a", "b"), text.take(3) is the first three characters, text.drop(3) is the rest, text.is_empty()
- To keep some items of a list: let big = for n in numbers where n > 10 { n }
- To put values into a text, write f before it: f"Total is {total} for {order.item}". Only Text and Numbers go inside the braces.
- No semicolons. No classes. No print. No null. No imports. A comment starts with //
- The entry is always: workflow main(input: In) -> Out { result = solve(input) }
- In and Out are records. solve is a function you write that takes In and returns Out.
- End with two or three tests. A test calls main with one record and checks fields of the answer.

EXAMPLE PROGRAM
record In { numbers: Number[] }
