// A second look at a worked-out answer before it is shown. A small model
// can take the right step and still say the wrong thing: "6pm is after
// 5pm" and then "yes, it is open". So the model called judge is given the
// same lines and question and works the answer out for itself, in writing,
// before it is shown the answer to compare with. Asked only for a verdict,
// a small model calls right answers wrong as often as not; made to do the
// work first, it does much better.
fn check_text(msg: Msg, worked: Worked, hits: Hits) -> Ask {
  let second = proof(hits, worked.also)
  let more = if worked.also.trim().is_empty() or second.is_empty() { "" } else { "\n" + line_of(second) }
  return {text: "Lines from a file:\n" + line_of(proof(hits, worked.quote)) + more + "\n\nQuestion: " + msg.text.trim() + "\n\nFirst, in working, answer the question yourself from the lines, step by step, using only the lines, plain arithmetic and what ordinary words mean. A place that closes at 5pm is closed at 6pm.\nIn answer, give your own answer in a few words: the number, or yes or no, or the name.\nThen compare your own answer with this proposed answer: " + worked.answer.trim() + "\nSet agrees to true if the proposed answer says the same as yours, and false if it says something different or the lines cannot answer the question."}
}

